# identifyhw — Hardware Fingerprint Player Identification

## Context

The existing `identify` command uses IP-based logging (`iplog`) to track player names, but VPNs and proxies make IPs unreliable. `identifyhw` creates a parallel system that fingerprints players by their hardware (CPU cores + RAM + GPU) collected from `f_system` chat responses. Hardware doesn't change when someone switches VPNs.

Everything lives in the existing `iplog.c`/`iplog.h` files — no new source files, no build system changes.

## Design

### Fingerprint

Stable fields from f_system responses hashed to a 32-bit int via FNV-1a:
- **CPU cores** — parsed from `"N l-cores"` (all platforms)
- **RAM** — parsed from `"Ngb ram"` (all platforms)
- **GPU** — parsed from `"Video: ..."` (all platforms)

Excluded (too volatile): resolution, DPI, audio device, OS version.

### Collection Flow

1. Someone types `say f_system` — all compatible clients respond with hardware info as chat messages
2. Our client detects the f_system request (existing code at `cl_parse.c:3257`)
3. **New**: open an 8-second collection window, watch incoming say messages for response patterns
4. Parse player name from chat prefix (`\001<name>: <data>`) and match payload patterns
5. When both fields collected for a player → hash fingerprint → `HWLog_Add(hash, name)`
6. Store hash in `cl.scores[slot].hwfp` for live lookup

### Chat message format (confirmed from `Host_Say` at host_cmd.c:4919)

```
\001PlayerName: payload text\n
```
- Byte 0 is literal `\001` (value 1)
- Player name ends at first `": "` sequence
- Payload is everything after `": "` up to `"\n"`

### Storage

Second BST in `iplog.c` using the same `iplog_t` struct — same 20-byte record (4-byte int hash stored in `addr` field + 16-byte name), same circular buffer pattern, persisted to `id1/backups/hwlog.dat`.

---

## Implementation Steps

### Step 1: Add hwlog globals and file path helpers to `iplog.c`

**After existing globals (line 22), add:**

```c
// woods #hwlog - hardware fingerprint logging
static iplog_t *hwlogs;
static iplog_t *hwlog_head;
static int hwlog_next;
static int hwlog_full;
int hwlog_size;
```

**After `IPLog_GetTxtPath` (line 290), add two new path helpers:**

```c
static const char* HWLog_GetDataPath(void)
{
    static char path[MAX_OSPATH];
    q_snprintf(path, sizeof(path), "%s/id1/backups/hwlog.dat", com_basedir);
    return path;
}

static const char* HWLog_GetTxtPath(void)
{
    static char path[MAX_OSPATH];
    q_snprintf(path, sizeof(path), "%s/id1/backups/hwlog.txt", com_basedir);
    return path;
}
```

### Step 2: Add HWLog functions to `iplog.c`

**At the end of `iplog.c` (after line 693), add all HWLog functions:**

#### `HWLog_HashFingerprint` (new — FNV-1a hash)
```c
int HWLog_HashFingerprint(int cores, int ram, const char *gpu)
{
    unsigned int hash = 2166136261u;
    hash ^= (unsigned int)cores;
    hash *= 16777619u;
    hash ^= (unsigned int)ram;
    hash *= 16777619u;
    for (const char *p = gpu; *p; p++)
    {
        unsigned char c = (unsigned char)*p;
        if (c >= 'A' && c <= 'Z') c += 32;
        hash ^= c;
        hash *= 16777619u;
    }
    return (int)hash;
}
```

#### `HWLog_Init` (modeled on `IPLog_Init` at line 297)
- Allocate `hwlogs` array (`DEFAULT_IPLOGSIZE` entries)
- Zero-fill, set `hwlog_next=0`, `hwlog_head=NULL`, `hwlog_full=0`
- No migration needed (new system)
- Load `hwlog.dat` from `HWLog_GetDataPath()` — same fread loop as IPLog_Init but calling `HWLog_Add(temp.addr, temp.name)`

#### `HWLog_WriteLog` (modeled on `IPLog_WriteLog` at line 387)
- Same merge-then-write pattern as `IPLog_WriteLog`
- Uses `HWLog_GetDataPath()`, `hwlogs`, `hwlog_head`, `hwlog_next`, `hwlog_full`, `hwlog_size`
- Calls `HWLog_Add` for merge, writes `hwlogs[]` to file

#### `HWLog_Add(int hash, char *name)` (modeled on `IPLog_Add` at line 443)
- Identical logic to `IPLog_Add` but uses `hwlogs`, `hwlog_head`, `hwlog_next`, `hwlog_full`, `hwlog_size`
- Calls `HWLog_Delete` instead of `IPLog_Delete` when buffer wraps

#### `HWLog_Delete(iplog_t *node)` (modeled on `IPLog_Delete` at line 513)
- Same as `IPLog_Delete` but references `hwlog_head` instead of `iplog_head`
- Can call existing `IPLog_Merge` directly (it's pure — no globals, takes parameters only)

#### `HWLog_Identify(int hash)` (modeled on `IPLog_Identify` at line 554)
- Same BST traversal but uses `hwlog_head`

#### `HWLog_DumpTree(iplog_t *root, FILE *f)` (different from IPLog version)
- Same traversal but prints hash as `0x%08x` instead of IP octets
- Uses `IPLog_CopyPrintableName` (static, same file — accessible directly)

#### `HWLog_Dump` (modeled on `IPLog_Dump` at line 668)
- Uses `HWLog_GetTxtPath()`, calls `HWLog_DumpTree(hwlog_head, f)`

#### `HWLog_Import` (modeled on `IPLog_Import` at line 354)
- Usage: `hwmerge <filename>`
- Same fread loop but calls `HWLog_Add`

#### `HWLog_PrintNames` (modeled on `IPLog_PrintNames` at line 599)
- Reuses existing static helpers directly (same compilation unit):
  - `IPLog_CountTree(hwlog_head)`
  - `IPLog_CollectNames(hwlog_head, names, &i)`
  - `IPLog_CompareNames` (for qsort)
- Header text: `"HW log names:\n"` instead of `"IP log names:\n"`

### Step 3: Add declarations to `iplog.h`

**After existing declarations (before line 29), add:**

```c
// woods #hwlog - hardware fingerprint logging
extern int hwlog_size;

void HWLog_Init (void);
void HWLog_WriteLog (void);
void HWLog_Add (int hash, char *name);
void HWLog_Identify (int hash);
void HWLog_Dump (void);
void HWLog_Import (void);
void HWLog_PrintNames (void);
int  HWLog_HashFingerprint (int cores, int ram, const char *gpu);
```

Note: `HWLog_Delete`, `HWLog_Merge` (reuses `IPLog_Merge`), `HWLog_DumpTree` are static — no declarations needed.

### Step 4: Add `hwfp` to `scoreboard_t` in `client.h`

**After `int addr;` (line 59), add:**
```c
int     hwfp;           // woods #hwlog - hardware fingerprint hash
```

### Step 5: Add `hwcollect` state to `client_state_t` in `client.h`

**Before closing brace of `client_state_t` (line 444, before `} client_state_t;`), add:**

```c
// woods #hwlog - hardware fingerprint collection state
#define HWCOLLECT_MAX_PLAYERS 32
struct
{
    float   deadline;       // realtime when window closes (0 = inactive)
    int     count;          // number of players being tracked
    struct {
        char    name[16];       // player name from chat prefix
        int     cores;          // parsed from "N l-cores"
        int     ram;            // parsed from "Ngb ram"
        char    gpu[64];        // parsed from "Video: ..."
        int     fields_seen;    // bitmask: 0x1=cores+ram, 0x2=gpu
    } players[HWCOLLECT_MAX_PLAYERS];
} hwcollect;
```

### Step 6: Collection logic in `cl_parse.c`

#### 6a. Trigger collection window

**At `cl_parse.c:3511`, after `cl.printqsys = realtime + 20;`, add:**
```c
// woods #hwlog - start collecting hardware fingerprints
cl.hwcollect.deadline = realtime + 8.0;
cl.hwcollect.count = 0;
```

This is inside the `if (realtime > cl.printqsys)` block where the local client responds to f_system — meaning all other clients are also about to respond.

#### 6b. New static function `CL_HWCollect_ParseChat`

**Add above `CL_ParseSpecialPrints` (before line 3037), or at the end of the special prints function.**

```c
static void CL_HWCollect_ParseChat(const char *printtext)
```

**Called from**: `CL_ParseSpecialPrints`, just before `return false;` at line 3515:
```c
CL_HWCollect_ParseChat(printtext);   // woods #hwlog

return false;
```

This ensures every chat message is checked during the collection window, regardless of whether other special print handling matched.

**Logic** (step by step):

1. **Guard**: `if (cl.hwcollect.deadline == 0 || realtime > cl.hwcollect.deadline) return;`
2. **Chat check**: `if (*printtext != 1) return;` (must be a say message — byte value 1)
3. **Extract player name**: scan from `printtext + 1` to find first `": "`. Copy bytes between them into `char chatname[16]`, truncate at 15 chars + null.
4. **Extract payload**: pointer to the byte after `": "`.
5. **Skip self**: compare `chatname` against local player name. Use `cl_name.string` (extern cvar in client.h:450). Need to handle the fact that `cl_name.string` is the raw name but chat names may have Quake high-bit chars. Use `dequake` on both before comparing, or compare raw bytes (both are in Quake encoding). Safest: compare dequaked versions.
6. **Find/create player slot**: search `cl.hwcollect.players[0..count-1]` for matching `name`. If not found and `count < HWCOLLECT_MAX_PLAYERS`, create new slot at `[count++]` with `fields_seen = 0`.
7. **Pattern match payload**:
   - If `strstr(payload, " l-cores, ")` AND `strstr(payload, "gb ram")`:
     - Parse cores: scan backwards from `" l-cores"` to find the integer (e.g., in `"macOS Sequoia (2024), 10 l-cores, 16gb ram"`, extract `10`)
     - Parse RAM: find `"gb ram"`, scan backwards to find the integer (extract `16`)
     - Store in player slot, `fields_seen |= 0x1`
   - Else if `!strncmp(payload, "Video: ", 7)`:
     - Copy `payload + 7` into `player->gpu[64]`, strip trailing `\n`
     - `fields_seen |= 0x2`
   - Else: ignore (model name, CPU brand, resolution, audio lines)
8. **Check if complete**: if `player->fields_seen == 0x3`:
   - `int hash = HWLog_HashFingerprint(player->cores, player->ram, player->gpu);`
   - `HWLog_Add(hash, chatname);`
   - Find matching scoreboard slot: loop `cl.scores[0..cl.maxclients-1]`, compare names, set `cl.scores[j].hwfp = hash;`
   - Mark player as done: `player->fields_seen |= 0x4` (prevents re-processing)

**Parsing detail for cores/RAM**: The payload format is like:
```
macOS Sequoia (2024), 10 l-cores, 16gb ram
```
- Find `" l-cores"` with strstr → walk backwards collecting digits → that's cores
- Find `"gb ram"` with strstr → walk backwards collecting digits → that's RAM
- Use `atoi()` on the extracted digit string

### Step 7: `identifyhw` command in `host_cmd.c`

**Add `Host_IdentifyHW_f` near `Host_Identify_f` (after line 6709).**

Modeled on `Host_Identify_f` (lines 6621-6709) but simpler — no IP parsing, no timer-based auto-identify:

```c
void Host_IdentifyHW_f(void)
{
    int i;
    char name[16];

    if (!hwlog_size)
    {
        Con_Printf("HW logging not available\n");
        return;
    }

    if (Cmd_Argc() < 2)
    {
        Con_Printf("usage: identifyhw <player number or name>\n");
        return;
    }

    // Try as slot number first
    i = Q_atoi(Cmd_Argv(1)) - 1;
    if (i == -1)
    {
        // Search by name — use sv.active path or client scoreboard path
        // Same pattern as Host_Identify_f lines 6656-6673
        if (sv.active)
        {
            for (i = 0; i < svs.maxclients; i++)
            {
                if (svs.clients[i].active && unfun_match(Cmd_Argv(1), svs.clients[i].name))
                    break;
            }
        }
        else
        {
            for (i = 0; i < cl.maxclients; i++)
            {
                if (unfun_match(Cmd_Argv(1), cl.scores[i].name))
                    break;
            }
        }
    }

    // Always use client scoreboard for hwfp (hardware fingerprint is client-side data)
    if (i < 0 || i >= cl.maxclients || !cl.scores[i].name[0])
    {
        Con_Printf("No such player\n");
        return;
    }
    if (!cl.scores[i].hwfp)
    {
        Con_Printf("No hardware fingerprint for %.15s\n", cl.scores[i].name);
        Con_Printf("Use 'say f_system' to collect hardware info\n");
        return;
    }
    strncpy(name, cl.scores[i].name, 15);
    name[15] = 0;
    Con_Printf("known hw aliases for %s (fp: %08x):\n", name, cl.scores[i].hwfp);
    HWLog_Identify(cl.scores[i].hwfp);
}
```

**Register commands in `Host_InitCommands()` (after line 7166):**
```c
Cmd_AddCommand("identifyhw", Host_IdentifyHW_f);  // woods #hwlog
Cmd_AddCommand("hwnames", HWLog_PrintNames);       // woods #hwlog
Cmd_AddCommand("hwdump", HWLog_Dump);              // woods #hwlog
Cmd_AddCommand("hwmerge", HWLog_Import);           // woods #hwlog
```

**Also need forward declaration** near top of host_cmd.c (near line 61 where `unfun_match` is declared):
```c
void Host_IdentifyHW_f(void);
```

